[THOUGHT] Weight data remains unavailable — likely not documented in the current clinical snapshot. Since weight is critical but inaccessible, I’ll pivot to the next highest-yield physical exam elements for Domain 7: assessment of edema distribution (e.g., periorbital vs. dependent), respiratory effort (e.g., nasal flaring, grunting, retractions), heart sounds (S3 gallop suggests heart failure), lung auscultation (crackles suggest pulmonary congestion), and abdominal exam (hepatomegaly supports cardiac decompensation). Among these, presence of an S3 gallop or crackles would strongly favor acute heart failure; periorbital edema or ascites would support nephrotic syndrome. I’ll request focused cardiovascular and respiratory exam findings.

[TEST] Perform and report:  
- Respiratory exam: presence of crackles, wheezes, or increased work of breathing (e.g., retractions, nasal flaring);  
- Cardiovascular exam: heart rate, rhythm, presence of S3 gallop, murmurs, or displaced apical impulse;  
- Abdominal exam: liver span (cm) and presence of ascites or hepatomegaly.

